ers from  common ones like anxiety and depression  to rare conditions like dementia and in  ivan's case  psychosis it was absolutely the most  terrifying thing i've ever experienced  in my life  what would you say then is the turning  point two weeks ago when i started  feeling  really good after i had my vaccination  ivan's back at work but still worries  how long is this going to stay with me  he hopes by sharing his story others  will also find the courage  to ask for help for cbs this morning ian  lee  in london and psychiatrist dr  sue varma joins us now sue good morning  this is uh  this was such a disturbing study uh when  i initially read the story  what's the biggest impact covet is  having on mental health  we're seeing that people 13 of people  who have never had psychiatric illnesses  before  neurological problems before are getting  this for the first time  and we're seeing anxiety being the most  common followed by depression followed  by insomnia  substance abuse and in very rare cases  even psychosis  who's at risk the people that are most  at risk are the ones who were the  sickest the hospitalized patients the  ones  who were in the icu those are the ones  who are more at risk for the more  serious conditions that we talked about  the strokes and the dementia and the  delirium which is a waxing and waning of  consciousness  but the scariest part are people who  didn't even have  either coveted symptoms or very mild  coveted symptoms that resolved  or prior psychiatric known illnesses  they too are at risk for the anxiety and  the depression and you know it's hard to  be able to tease apart is it because of  the hospitalization and just the fear  and the economic impact and all the  layers  of the pandemic or was there something  also medical going on and that's what  this  study picked up that there was something  medical going on in the brain right  so i mean we're talking about people who  have never experienced symptoms like  this before  in many cases precisely and that is so  scary especially when you're hearing  voices or the voices are telling you to  harm yourself or harm other people or  that other people are out to get you  so um these type of symptoms are they  ever linked to other kinds of viral  infections  you know they are but you know not to  the manifestation not to the extent that  it is when  they compared in this study to uh covet  the flu or code it to uh  other control groups or other  respiratory symptoms you saw that uh the  covet was twice as likely to produce  these neuropsychiatric  problems as compared to the flu so this  is something really unique  and the fact that people could have mild  coveted symptoms mild respiratory  symptoms and yet  major mental health and major  neuropsychiatric problems i i noted that  ivan said  in in the story that that he started  feeling better after he got the  vaccination  what what should people do in in this  case  yeah so the first thing is never  underestimate or minimize your uh  mental health concerns because they very  much may be linked to the inflammation  that cobot caused in the body you know i  think of covid really as a  multi-organ system illnesses many  doctors understand this because of the  blood vessels that are injured  we have inflammation neurotoxins in the  brain um  and we need to be able to talk to both  the primary care doctors sometimes  they're  long-term care centers dedicated to  covet and also speak to a behavioral  health specialist and finally talk to  your family about developing a care plan  a lot of people are having brain fog  memory problems  it's affecting their people's ability to  function at home and functioning the  family so really be able to have a  comprehensive plan with the primary care  doctor your family and  a behavioral health specialist as we saw  for many people these things are  lingering dr  sue varma thank you so much
 

